const PROJECT_RULES = 'Project rules: read and follow whichever of these exist in the project: CLAUDE.md, .claude/CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md, AGENTS.md, .claude/rules/*.md, plus any files they @-import; in a monorepo also the CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md of parent folders up to the repo root.'
// After an outside wait a request re-checks access and existence with the request's own clock reading (round tg: an advisor's design took a fresh one); a changed input is by itself never an error and never a reason to call again (round tj: both judges read a second call as behaviour the spec does not state). Time limits are checked twice.
const HOUSE_RULES = "House rules (the user's standards): return new objects instead of mutating; small focused files (200–400 lines typical, 800 max) and functions (under 50 lines); validate outside input at boundaries (never replies from the project's own server beyond what the spec states; never refuse input the spec accepts, and add no step the spec does not state, such as stripping a BOM, trimming or case-folding, that lets through input its steps reject); data other tools can write (a shared database, a file) may hold any value its store allows: read and reply with every such value without failing or dropping it (in JavaScript, read integer columns that can pass 2^53, such as ids, as BigInt), a row that points at a row that does not exist included: where the spec says what an id of no existing row gives (it matches nothing, it is not found), that holds even when other rows still carry that id; a request that reads data, waits on an outside call that changes nothing outside (a model, also one run on this machine, a lookup) and then writes or replies from that data checks again, in the transaction that writes, that the user still has access and the data still exists: if not, it writes nothing and replies as the spec says for a missing one, because access is decided when the write happens and a write for something deleted fits no one-at-a-time order; that call is made once (its attempts as the spec counts them; where it counts none, the client's default), with no transaction open, and if what it sent has changed in the store meanwhile and the spec names no reply for that, the request ends as the spec says for a good answer, with that answer (the change is no error, and no reason to call again unless the spec says so), where the project has docs they say the answer is for the data as read, and where the write stores the answer itself beside that data, it never replaces an answer stored from newer data than it was sent (it keeps beside it the version of that data it came from, such as a revision or an updated time, and checks and writes in one statement), and you name that under Next in your hand-back; a value the spec fixes once for a request (its time, an id, a token) is taken once and passed on: the re-check after a wait, the write and each retry use it, never a fresh one (elapsed-time checks on a monotonic clock are separate); a time limit the spec sets on an outside call is enforced twice: a timer that aborts the call and stays set until the whole reply body is read, and the elapsed time on a monotonic clock (performance.now() in JavaScript) since the attempt began, checked again when the reply completes; handle errors explicitly and never swallow them; user-facing errors stay friendly and leak nothing (one line, no stack trace); no hardcoded secrets; a number with a meaning has a name; no console.log in shipped code; names, comments and docs in shipped files say what the product does, never how the work was done: no word for a team section, a review, a probe or a fix round, and nothing that points into `.team/`; write tests first for new behaviour and aim for 80% coverage; a time limit in a test (a process start, an awaited event, the test's own timeout) only catches a hang and never measures speed: set it far above the usual time (a minute costs nothing when the test passes) and wait for the event itself, never for a fixed delay; assert a duration only against a time the spec names, with a margin that is a fraction of that time, and show that something is answered meanwhile by order (its reply comes before the reply of the request that waits), never by a duration; a time another process stored (a server the test starts) is never held against the test's own clock without a margin of about a second, because clocks read in two processes can differ by a few milliseconds: compare it with another value that process stored, or allow that margin; before you add a test, look at the tests that exist: reuse their helpers and stand-ins, put the case in the file that covers that feature, and write no case another test already covers; conventional commit messages if you commit."
// Builders and the coder only: Bash output is 27% of head context.
const SHORT_OUTPUT = 'Keep tool output short: run tests with a reporter that ends with pass and fail totals (never a dots-only one, also in the test script you give the project) and read its last lines through `tail`; run the full suite once per change and take totals and coverage from that one run, coverage from the project\'s own coverage setup or the test runner\'s own option: where neither sees part of the code (code that runs only in a process the tests start), report what it does measure with the line `coverage of started processes not measured`, and write no tool of your own to measure it; pipe other long output through `tail`, and never print a whole log. Everything you read is re-sent on every later turn.'
// Builders only: their allowlist leaves out project MCP servers (see BUILDER_TOOLS).
const NO_PROJECT_MCP = 'Your tools include no project MCP servers (a database console, a cloud API): for one of those, use its CLI through Bash, or ask `main` to run the call.'
// Builders and the coder. Round tg: probe scripts were left beside the project and in the home folder, and two sections tried to stop every process of one name. Real use, 2026-11-11: product files changed in place to watch a test fail, and DOM test runs with no limit, came before three out-of-memory crashes.
const TIDY = 'Scratch files (probe scripts, logs) go in `.team/tmp/<section id>/` under your working folder (your own id, or your head\'s when you are a helper). The plugin makes that folder before each tool call of a section, so do not make or remove it; if a write there ever fails because it is missing, add `mkdir -p` to that same command. Copies of the project go in the system temp folder, never beside the project or in the home folder. Before you hand back, remove your scratch files and your copies by their paths in one command; never search the temp folder for what other runs left, and make every test remove the temp files and folders it makes. Stop only the processes you started, by their PID, never by name (`taskkill /IM`, `pkill`, `killall`): other work runs on this machine. Other sections run tests on these same files, so never change a product file in place only to watch a test fail (a mutation check): make that change in a copy of the project in the system temp folder, or reason it through without running it. Give every test run a limit that stops it from outside: run it through Bash as `timeout <seconds> <command>` where the system has that command (in PowerShell `timeout` only pauses), set to a few times its usual run time, or a few minutes for a file you have not run yet. The per-test timeout of a test runner cannot stop a loop that never yields. A run that dies with no error of its own may have been stopped for its memory use: if `.team/tmp/memory-guard.log` exists it names the run, and you find the cause before you run it again.'

// The reviewer's clean-code rubric. Later tasks import RUBRIC_ITEMS and PASS_RULE.
export const RUBRIC_ITEMS = ['structure', 'naming', 'duplication', 'size', 'errors', 'tests', 'security', 'leftovers']
export const PASS_RULE = `Pass: overall at least ${PASS_OVERALL} and no item under 6.`
const RUBRIC_TEXT = {
  structure: 'one clear job per file and function, high cohesion and low coupling, no nesting deeper than 4 levels, new objects returned instead of mutation.',
  naming: 'descriptive names that say what a thing is or does, consistent with the surrounding code; a number with a meaning has a name.',
  duplication: 'no copy-pasted logic that should be one shared function, in tests too: a case another test already covers, or a second copy of a helper or stand-in the test folder has, is a fix.',
  size: 'files 200–400 lines typical and 800 max; functions under 50 lines.',
  errors: 'errors handled explicitly and never swallowed; outside input (requests, files, env, outside services) validated at boundaries, including every check the spec states; a type check on the project\'s own server\'s replies beyond what the spec states, or a refusal of input the spec accepts, is a fix to remove, and so is a step the spec does not state (a stripped BOM, a trim, a case-fold) that lets through input its steps reject; so is a size cap that adds a failure where the spec lists when something fails; a stored value its store allows that makes a read fail is a fix; user-facing errors stay friendly and leak nothing. At the process boundary (CLI main, server startup, request handler) a failure a user can cause ends as one plain line and a non-zero exit: OS errors are told apart by err.code (ENOENT, EISDIR, EACCES, EADDRINUSE), and other errors are never relabelled as I/O or data errors. Rethrowing to the runtime is not handling: a stack trace a user can trigger (a bad env value, a data path that is a directory, a missing parent folder, a port in use) scores errors under 6. A message or exit code the spec names stays exact. When the spec orders steps and two of them fail, the message of the step it puts first wins: running a later step\'s check earlier, so that its message wins, is a fix.',
  tests: 'new behaviour has tests, written first, and coverage is about 80% or better where the project\'s coverage setup or the test runner\'s own option measures it (code that runs only in a process the tests start is no fail for that); each requirement has a test that a plausible wrong implementation would fail (NFD instead of NFKD, trim instead of trimEnd, < instead of <=), not only the spec examples. A test that asserts behaviour a spec line contradicts is a fix, and so is a test that needs this checkout\'s own git history or .team/ contents (a source export or a shallow clone has neither): keep what it needs, such as a data file an older version made, as a fixture file in the test folder; a test that builds its own temporary repo or state is fine. A test a busy machine can fail is a fix too: a start or a wait limited near its usual time, a fixed delay in place of the event, a duration asserted where the spec names no time (the order of two replies is no duration), a time another process stored held against the test\'s own clock with no margin.',
  security: 'no hardcoded secrets, no injection or unsafe handling of user input, and no access or existence check made before an outside wait (a model or network call) and trusted after it: the code that writes or replies makes it again (content that changed meanwhile is by itself no error and no reason to call again, unless the spec says so: the answer is used (where it is stored beside the data it came from, it never replaces an answer stored from newer data), and an error reply for a change alone is a fix, so is a second call made for it, so is a call made while a transaction is open, and so is a test that expects any of these). A result the server worked out itself between a read and the write (a hash, anything awaited that is no outside call; a model run on this machine is an outside call) and used after the rows it came from have changed is a fix, a last fallback after retries included, and so is a check, such as a count of recent failures, whose outcome comes from a read made before that work and not from the transaction that writes; a change in a row only a check read is no reason to do the work again (where the work cannot run in its blocking form inside the write transaction on one synchronous connection and the store locks no rows, the reply for an unexpected failure after 3 rounds that each found the rows the work came from changed is no fix). A value the spec fixes once for a request (its time, an id, a token) that is taken again after a wait is a fix (an elapsed-time check on a monotonic clock is not that value), and so is a test of that wait that a second reading would pass. A check of a secret that does less work for an unknown name than for a wrong secret is a fix.',
  leftovers: 'no console.log, debug code, commented-out code or stray TODOs in shipped code, and no name, comment or doc line that tells how the work was done instead of what the code does (a team section, a review, a probe, a fix round, a `.team/` path; a test file named for the step that wrote it). No flag, hook or branch in shipped code that exists only to change behaviour under test (a dependency passed in, such as a clock, a client or a store, is design, not a leftover), no doc list or count (test files, totals) the folder no longer matches, and no scratch file or empty folder left in the project outside `.team/`.',
}

